# Expectation record for 05_pointers/005_function_pointers_basic.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 005_function_pointers_basic
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Function pointer declaration, assignment and indirect call in both the fp(...) and (*fp)(...) forms
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every function pointer is assigned a function of exactly the matching type before it is
called, so no call is ever made through an incompatible type. The null function pointer is
compared and tested for truth but NEVER called. All arithmetic performed inside the called
functions is on small values well inside the range of int, so no signed overflow occurs;
the largest product computed is 120. The fold reads only the five initialized elements of
data.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No function address is printed. Function-pointer facts appear only as equality
comparisons against the corresponding function designators and as the results of the calls.
No address or pointer value is printed either, and this program forms no pointer
difference at all, so the measured i686 width differences (a 4-byte long and pointer
against 8 bytes on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64, and a 32- rather than 64-bit ptrdiff_t)
cannot surface in its output.
Plain char is never printed in a signedness-dependent way; no char is printed at all, so
the measured char signedness difference (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64
and riscv64) is irrelevant. The only conversion specifier used anywhere in this program
is %d, which is target-width independent.
END
